{
  "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80f9-84a5-fd65e9263256",
  "title": "Creating Executable JARs and Deploying Spring Boot Apps",
  "sourceUrl": "https://xpandeyed.notion.site/20924eb1ed5480f984a5fd65e9263256",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80b6-a3cf-c0b0d338adfc",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-803c-ac0e-e51f3799fdbe",
      "type": "header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Creating Executable JARs and Deploying Spring Boot Apps"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-808f-acca-f9dd3e020c2e",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8038-a8b6-f10b900556ea",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "1. Core Concept"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80c0-9539-e96d963a9e3a",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "An "
        ],
        [
          "executable JAR",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " (Java ARchive) is a packaged Java application bundled with all the code and libraries needed to run independently. Spring Boot automatically builds these executable JARs to simplify deploying Java applications—so you don’t have to worry about manually managing dependencies or application servers. You just run the JAR with a simple command, and your app starts up anywhere Java is installed."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-806f-8ec9-dae5f7b28b51",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8086-9a85-fb4a836afe28",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "2. Key Interview Points"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8042-8007-e09d9217d2f6",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "What is an executable JAR and why is it useful?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "20924eb1-ed54-809c-9ba2-ca49280169da",
          "type": "text",
          "richText": [
            [
              "It packages your app + all dependencies into one file, making deployment easier."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-804e-8f48-e5af9dc5e1f0",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "How does Spring Boot build an executable JAR?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80ca-9969-f18aaf6e455b",
          "type": "text",
          "richText": [
            [
              "Spring Boot uses a plugin (like Maven or Gradle) to bundle everything, including an embedded server like Tomcat, so you don’t need a separate web server."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80e4-82a3-d6d0945d15a1",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "How do you run and deploy a Spring Boot executable JAR?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80bd-ad53-e679b18257c4",
          "type": "text",
          "richText": [
            [
              "Using "
            ],
            [
              "java -jar yourapp.jar",
              [
                [
                  "c"
                ]
              ]
            ],
            [
              " on any system with a compatible JVM installed."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8085-adc4-d887985d0a4a",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "What is the difference between a WAR and an executable JAR?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8081-a609-e40864c89eab",
          "type": "text",
          "richText": [
            [
              "WAR files are traditional Java web app archives deployed on external servers, whereas Spring Boot executable JARs contain the server inside them (self-contained)."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8028-92b8-ffeb7ce8baa6",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8022-b39b-e991034a9799",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "3. Code Example"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8046-b31c-c9e54236bfd8",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Here’s a simple Spring Boot app and how you configure it to produce an executable JAR:"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8037-9610-e7f4e836997a",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Step 1: Basic Spring Boot Application",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-805d-a123-e2233e43e6ce",
      "type": "code",
      "richText": [
        [
          "import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;\nimport org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;\n\n@SpringBootApplication  // Marks this as a Spring Boot app\npublic class DemoApplication {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args); // Bootstraps the app\n    }\n}\n"
        ]
      ],
      "language": "Java"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8052-b62d-c90af124c6cc",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Step 2: Maven ",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "pom.xml",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ],
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " for executable JAR",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80fd-8144-f710f9f78e35",
      "type": "code",
      "richText": [
        [
          "<project>\n    <!-- other config omitted for brevity -->\n    <dependencies>\n        <!-- Spring Boot Starter for web apps -->\n        <dependency>\n            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>\n        </dependency>\n    </dependencies>\n\n    <build>\n        <plugins>\n            <plugin>\n                <!-- Spring Boot Maven Plugin -->\n                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>\n                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>\n                <version>3.0.0</version> <!-- use appropriate version -->\n                <executions>\n                    <execution>\n                        <goals>\n                            <goal>repackage</goal> <!-- creates executable JAR -->\n                        </goals>\n                    </execution>\n                </executions>\n            </plugin>\n        </plugins>\n    </build>\n</project>\n"
        ]
      ],
      "language": "XML"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8037-974f-f6231374384e",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Step 3: Build and run",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80ec-9a8e-e2295bf95de7",
      "type": "code",
      "richText": [
        [
          "mvn clean package            # Builds the executable JAR in target/\njava -jar target/demo.jar    # Runs the app anywhere with Java installed\n"
        ]
      ],
      "language": "Shell"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80b0-85d3-c2041678e112",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80de-acf1-e26b8d78c644",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "4. Common Interview Questions"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-806c-8b20-f3b31fc65ded",
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "What is an executable JAR and how does Spring Boot create it?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8046-9b65-c2e0bc0971b1",
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "How do you run a Spring Boot application packaged as an executable JAR?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80d4-be38-e4d487eb039b",
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "What is the role of the Spring Boot Maven or Gradle plugin?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80f4-bb8e-fc96075888b0",
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Why might you choose an executable JAR over a WAR file for deployment?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8088-9a35-c07ff5b03e54",
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "How does Spring Boot handle embedded web servers in executable JARs?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8064-8fc4-c42426cdc455",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-803d-941d-ff070999d67e",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "5. Best Practices & Pitfalls"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8001-b069-cbceae3a01bb",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Best Practices:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8040-80a1-f62428bae133",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Always use Spring Boot’s build plugins (Maven/Gradle) to package your app — they handle dependencies and server bundling automatically."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8014-89e1-eaf1c36b12f5",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Keep your application’s external configuration (e.g., "
        ],
        [
          "application.properties",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ") separate from the JAR, so you can change settings without rebuilding."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-801b-93b4-f42844c628ab",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Test your JAR on your target deployment environment to ensure it runs as expected."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80d8-979a-f5f157270ff1",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Common Pitfalls:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-800d-836a-c97cbe3c1ecd",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Trying to run the JAR without a compatible Java runtime installed on the deployment machine."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80b6-badf-d8f0e2900060",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Forgetting to include the Spring Boot plugin in your build file, resulting in a non-executable JAR."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80b1-8cd6-de534aa7a160",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Bundling unnecessary large dependencies, making the JAR size too big."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80c4-8bb9-f5221641ad09",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80a0-9076-dafc8b335d6f",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "6. Connection to Other Concepts"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-803c-b88f-d206c2729aec",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Beans & Auto-configuration:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " The executable JAR includes your Spring beans and auto-configuration classes, enabling your app’s components to start automatically."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8046-b4b4-ca0a002f014e",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Classpath:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " The JAR bundles all dependencies inside, so the classpath is managed internally — no external setup needed."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8087-ad12-fb70ec5b7d7c",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Profiles & Configuration:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " You can supply different configurations at runtime (like dev or prod profiles) even when running the executable JAR."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-808c-a2ab-c21ce2c0a88c",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Embedded Server:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " This topic ties directly into how Spring Boot embeds servers like Tomcat or Jetty inside the JAR."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8082-8a5f-db128100d067",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80ea-a6bd-fc58b0fd87f8",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "7. Quick Interview Answers"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80a3-b70d-e243eba7fe78",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: What is an executable JAR in Spring Boot?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-809d-b191-ffad08368726",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "A: It’s a Java archive file packaged by Spring Boot that contains your application code, all dependencies, and an embedded web server, allowing you to run your app with a simple "
        ],
        [
          "java -jar",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " command without needing external servers."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-807f-ab97-ec0be3aabf6b",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: How do you create an executable JAR in Spring Boot?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80ad-a20a-f704f05d86da",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "A: By including the Spring Boot Maven or Gradle plugin in your build configuration, then running the build command ("
        ],
        [
          "mvn package",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " or "
        ],
        [
          "gradle build",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "), which packages your app and dependencies into a runnable JAR."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8057-85d1-d2c5bb21d939",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: How is deploying a Spring Boot executable JAR different from a traditional Java web app?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8070-a75d-f5e173a6d6a7",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "A: Unlike traditional WAR files that need an external server like Tomcat, executable JARs have an embedded server bundled inside, so deployment is as easy as copying and running a single file."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8095-b209-e273c1ffcaf2",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-deployment-paths",
      "type": "header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Choose a deployment path"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-deployment-paths-b0",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "The executable JAR is the packaged application. The host still needs a compatible Java runtime, configuration, network access and a process-management plan."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-deployment-paths-b1",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Managed hosting handles parts of the machine and process environment. A VPS gives more control and also leaves more server maintenance with you."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-deployment-paths-b2",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Read the managed-hosting page for assigned ports and release configuration. Read the VPS page for systemd, the reverse proxy and HTTPS."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-deployment-paths-b3",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Verify build, startup, public routing, authentication and database operations separately. Success at one boundary does not prove the others."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-cloud-link",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Managed hosting",
          [
            [
              "a",
              "/notes/spring-boot/deployment/cloud"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-vps-link",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "VPS deployment",
          [
            [
              "a",
              "/notes/spring-boot/deployment/vps"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    }
